WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.600
It's hard to imagine
but just 300 years ago,

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.160
it was widely believed that insects
spontaneously spawned

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.920
from dust, mud or rotten meat.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.200
Their reproduction was felt to be
so strange and frightening,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.600
they were known as
beasts of the devil.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.520
It's partly thanks to the work
of naturalist and illustrator,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.360
Maria Sibylla Merian,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.200
that we can now find
this idea amusing.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.920
Today Merian is celebrated as one
of the world's first ecologists.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.400
She is admired by
Sir David Attenborough,

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.240
who described her as one of the most
significant contributors

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.960
to entomology,
the study of insects.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.880
But she faced many challenges in her
journey to educate the world,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.960
not least because of her sex.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.840
Born into a family of artists
in Frankfurt in 1647,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.560
Merian spent her childhood
collecting insects

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.920
and drawing them
in remarkable detail.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.640
In 1665, she married
and soon had two daughters.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.080
In an age where a woman's place
was thought to be in the home,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.200
she defied convention,

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.400
publishing her first book,
New Book of Flowers, in 1675.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.560
Merian was one
of the first to describe

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.920
the metamorphosis of insects
in detail,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.920
declaring that all moths
and butterflies

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.640
hatch from eggs after reproduction
and do not just magically appear.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.120
In 1699,
Merian, by then divorced,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.760
sold her belongings and set sail
to Suriname in South America

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.880
with one of her daughters,
Dorothea.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.520
She's thought to be the first woman
to travel in the name of science.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.360
After a long sea journey,
they set off into the jungle,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.400
battling through thick clouds
of black flies

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.520
that inflicted skin-ripping bites -
but it was worth it.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.360
The jungles of Suriname were
a naturalists' paradise,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.840
teeming with species that would
later be documented

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.120
in Merian's groundbreaking work,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.880
'The Metamorphosis of the Insects
of Suriname', published in 1705.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.920
Its stunning illustrations depicted
stages of development of Suriname's

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.720
veracious caterpillars
and vibrant butterflies,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.040
as well as many other creatures
from this exotic habitat.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.800
Uniquely for the time,
she also portrayed insects surrounded

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.520
by the plants they relied on,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.600
revealing their relationship
to the wider ecosystem.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.000
She was the first to show that the
change from caterpillar to butterfly

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.040
depended on a number of plants.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.960
Though she had planned
to stay longer,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.360
Merian's adventure
was cut short by illness

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.960
and after two years
she returned to Amsterdam.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.440
Given the challenges of working in
a humid and unfamiliar environment,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.640
it is remarkable how many species
she was able to observe

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.480
and catalogue in that time.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.120
Her writing is also notable for
acknowledging the mistreatment

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.400
of enslaved indigenous
and African people

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.520
and their contribution
to her research.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.680
She wrote in her description
of the peacock flower,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.240
'The Indians, who are not treated
well by their Dutch masters,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.840
use the seeds to abort
their children,

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.360
so that they will not become slaves
like themselves.'

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.840
Merian suffered a stroke in 1715
that left her unable to work.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.960
She died two years later, aged 69.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.440
However, Merian's legacy has endured.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.560
To date, at least six plants,
nine butterflies, two bugs,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.840
a spider and a lizard,
have been named after her.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.600
Despite being over 300 years old,
her work on the biodiversity

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.760
of Suriname is still valued
by scientists

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.960
and could show us how some species
may adapt to climate change.

